  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft remains over the Central Plains this afternoon, \nwedged between a mid-level ridge across the southeast CONUS and a \nclosed low over northern California. Yesterday's front has been \nlifting back north through the day today, allowing for skies to \nclear from south to north. Temperatures range from the low 90s \nacross east-central Kansas to the low 70s near the Kansas-Nebraska \nstate line. Light winds and high boundary layer moisture set the \nstage for fog development overnight, some of which could be\ndense. Coverage of fog and how dense fog will be remains uncertain;\nwill need to monitor observation trends overnight. Isentropic \nascent is expected to generate scattered showers and isolated \nstorms tonight into Thursday morning, mainly across north-central\nand northern Kansas. Fog and clouds will gradually lift through\nthe morning Thursday and afternoon sunshine will help boost \ntemperatures into the upper 80s to mid 90s. \n\nThe mid-level ridge retrogrades into Friday as the western US upper \nlow slides east into the Rockies. A deepening surface low stretching \nfrom central Nebraska into southeast Colorado will tighten the \npressure gradient over the forecast area. Given breezy southerly \nwinds, temperatures will likely over perform NBM deterministic \nsolutions and most locations should reach the low to mid 90s. \nDewpoints remain in the upper 60s to low 70s, creating afternoon \nheat indices of around 100 degrees. The surface trough may make it \nas far east as north-central Kansas by Friday evening. Sufficient \nconvergence along the boundary could exist to develop a couple of \nstorms in an uncapped and unstable environment. High-based \nconvection and inverted-V soundings would support some strong wind \ngusts if storms are able to develop.\n\nTroughing ejects across the Plains this weekend, pushing a cold \nfront into the area. Timing of the front is still varied among \nguidance, but the most likely timing of FROPA is Saturday \nevening/night. Best chances for precipitation will be associated \nwith frontal passage; the severe parameter space is progged to be at \nleast marginally favorable for some strong to severe storms along \nthe boundary. Chances for showers and storms continue into early \nnext week as more fall-like air works into the region. Highs in the \n70s with lows around 60 degrees are forecast Monday into the middle \nof next week.",
